comments.body:
> Check in StyleCheck only the diff. Does it need to be a block? Can't we decouple it and run in in parallel?

Useful, IMHO. The same applies to the spell check.

@participant3 tried it, as far as I remember.

@participant12 suggested to make these checks pre-commit hooks, IIRC.

I personally think typos and incorrect formatting are overrated. For me, it would be fine to not check these things at all. Instead, we could fix both 1x month as part of the monthly release workflow.

@reporter You are right of course, I mis-read `Only run tests if only tests are added/modified. Run them 1000 times in parallel`.

@participant3 Agree that it is not possible to find the right single job variant. It only makes sense to only run _all_ job variants + all flaky check jobs, each with a single execution of the changed test(s).

Re your second point: My experience (as a developer) is that flaky tests fail in 99% of all cases because of randomized session settings or MergeTree settings. Failure because of a certain execution sequence or because of tests running in parallel happen but they are rare. So I guess running just the changed test(s) will still be okay (and if not, then the developer could do a whitespace change in C++ code and trigger a full run).

There is another aspect to consider: The number of PRs that only change test(s) is a lot (!) smaller than the total number of PRs, I would estimate it as 5%. We could optimize the hell out of these 5% but the overall impact on CI costs and the developer experience will be limited. In that sense, I think we can discard this idea.

@participant2 

> Merge stateless and stateful test jobs into a single functional test job.

Thanks, I'm in favor of this. Just copy the files, and add `-- Tags: stateful`

@participant8, ThinLTO is not needed for development, that's why it is disabled by default. But it is needed for production, as it has a significant performance improvement.

I probably used ThinLTO on my machine once or twice over many years, and it does not bother me.
And in the CI, it is comparable to the time it takes to run integration tests - also not a big problem.
